What are fixtures?

Fixtures are reusuable functions that prepares the environments needed for a testcase(s) and optionally cleans up after execution is done.

It is liek a monolithic function 

eg;opening a browser , connecting to db. So basically it is a setup and clean up mechanism 

Imagine a cricket match.

Before the players start playing:

The pitch is prepared.The stumps are fixed.The ball is brought.The scoreboard is reset.The umpires are ready.The players don't prepare these things themselves—they simply come and play.

A fixture is like the ground staff. It prepares everything before the match and cleans up afterward.

Similarly, in Playwright:

Launch the browser
Open a page
Log in (if required)

The test only focuses on verifying functionality.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why do we use fixtures?

Fixtures provide several benefits:

♻️ Code reuse – Write setup once, use it in many tests.
🧹 Cleaner tests – Test functions contain only test logic.
🔧 Easy maintenance – Update setup in one place.
⚡ Consistent environment – Every test starts from a known state.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Explain fixtures and yield with example?

@pytest.fixture
def login_page(page):
  page.goto("www.google.com")
  page.fill("username","admin")
  page.fill("password","23434")
  page.click("button",type='submit'")
  yield page

def create_user(login_page):
  login_page.click("text="create_user")

Everything before yield is the set up and everything after the yield is tear down. 

